Patch 2066: the D3D12 backend never frees or reuses GPU objects under a batch that may still be running: the GPU wait
only gives up on a removed device, and the deferred-release table grows instead of freeing its oldest entry early.

Two places in rsx_d3d12_backend.c acted on "the GPU has surely finished" without knowing it:
- wait_for_gpu() stopped waiting after three 2 s timeouts while GetDeviceRemovedReason still said S_OK, and
  render_frame() then reset the command allocator, rewrote the upload rings and texture ring, and released evicted
  objects under a batch that was still executing: a corrupted command stream on a GPU that was merely slow (a loaded,
  shared GPU; a first-use shader-cache flush). It now waits as long as the device is alive (a [D3D12] STUCK line every
  2 s) and only a removed device, whose fence never signals, is given up on (the TDR case the bounded wait was for).
- The grave of patch 2028 (pipelines and shader blobs evicted from the caches, released two frames later after the
  drain) was a fixed table of 512; when full it released the oldest entry at once. After a mid-frame flush (patch 2003
  leaves that batch running) the oldest can still be in use: the GPU use-after-free the grave exists to prevent. The
  table now grows (realloc, doubling from 512); if memory runs out the object is kept, never freed early.

Found by reading code during a device-loss hunt; neither path was caught in the act. Every device loss seen on the test
PC was an NVIDIA Xid 69 class error, so this patch removes two latent faults, not that one. No behaviour change on a healthy run:
the wait returns on the first try and the grave never held more than a few hundred entries.

Source: written for v0.19.0 (2026-11-09). Upstream candidate (general: no title-specific code).

diff --git a/libs/video/rsx_d3d12_backend.c b/libs/video/rsx_d3d12_backend.c
--- a/libs/video/rsx_d3d12_backend.c
+++ b/libs/video/rsx_d3d12_backend.c
@@ -1534,15 +1534,20 @@
  * frame, or a command list still on the GPU, can use them. They wait here and go once render_frame has drained the
  * GPU two frames later (patch 2028). */
 typedef struct { IUnknown* obj; u64 frame; } D3DGrave;
-static D3DGrave s_grave[512];
-static int s_grave_n = 0;
+static D3DGrave* s_grave = NULL;
+static int s_grave_n = 0, s_grave_cap = 0;
 static void grave_add(IUnknown* o, u64 frame)
 {
     if (!o) return;
-    if (s_grave_n == (int)(sizeof s_grave / sizeof s_grave[0])) {   /* full: the oldest has surely finished by now */
-        s_grave[0].obj->lpVtbl->Release(s_grave[0].obj);
-        memmove(&s_grave[0], &s_grave[1], sizeof(D3DGrave) * (size_t)(s_grave_n - 1));
-        s_grave_n--;
+    if (s_grave_n == s_grave_cap) {
+        /* Patch 2066: grow, never release early. The old fixed table freed its oldest entry at once when full ("has
+         * surely finished"), which is false after a mid-frame flush (patch 2003 leaves that batch running on the GPU)
+         * and is exactly the GPU use-after-free the grave exists to prevent. Out of memory: keep the object (a leak,
+         * never a free under the GPU). */
+        int ncap = s_grave_cap ? s_grave_cap * 2 : 512;
+        D3DGrave* ng = (D3DGrave*)realloc(s_grave, sizeof(D3DGrave) * (size_t)ncap);
+        if (!ng) return;
+        s_grave = ng; s_grave_cap = ncap;
     }
     s_grave[s_grave_n].obj = o; s_grave[s_grave_n].frame = frame; s_grave_n++;
 }
@@ -1580,7 +1585,11 @@
          * Degrade loudly instead: bounded waits + the removal reason, then
          * carry on (subsequent D3D calls fail visibly but the game keeps
          * running headless). */
-        for (int tries = 0; tries < 3; tries++) {
+        /* Patch 2066: wait as long as the device is alive. The old loop gave up after three 2 s waits and the
+         * callers then reset the command allocator, rewrote the upload rings and released evicted objects under a
+         * batch that was still running: a corrupted command stream on a GPU that was merely slow (a loaded,
+         * shared GPU; a first-use cache flush). Only a removed device (its fence never signals) is given up on. */
+        for (int tries = 0;; tries++) {
             if (WaitForSingleObject(s_d3d.fence_event, 2000) != WAIT_TIMEOUT)
                 return;
             HRESULT rr = s_d3d.device->lpVtbl->GetDeviceRemovedReason(s_d3d.device);
@@ -1590,7 +1599,7 @@
                    (unsigned long long)s_d3d.fence->lpVtbl->GetCompletedValue(s_d3d.fence),
                    (long)rr);
             fflush(stdout);
-            if (rr != 0 /* S_OK: device still alive, keep waiting a bit */)
+            if (rr != 0 /* removed: nothing will ever signal, carry on headless */)
                 break;
         }
     }
